---
title: Data Management
---

Good data management keeps records understandable, recoverable, and useful
after fieldwork. Define conventions before collecting, review records during
curation, and preserve the context needed to interpret each project.

## Capture complete data

- Use stable identifiers for projects, sites, events, specimens, and parts.
- Use controlled values for people, methods, preparation types, and other
  repeated fields.
- Record dates, locations, collectors, methods, and uncertainty as soon as
  practical.
- Link specimens to their sites, events, personnel, taxonomy, media, and
  associated data.

## Review and clean records

Review records in the field before moving to the next specimen. In the lab,
check for duplicate identifiers, missing required values, invalid coordinates,
inconsistent dates, and broken media links. Normalize taxon names, personnel,
localities, and methods while keeping the original field information available
when it is needed to understand a correction.

Use a second person to review important changes. Record the project scope,
collection methods, responsible curators, and major corrections so another user
can understand how the dataset was created.

## Protect sensitive information

Identify sensitive locations, personal information, and restricted collection
details before sharing data. Export only what the recipient needs and use
secure storage for project archives and backups. Follow institutional policies
and applicable collecting, access, and data-sharing requirements.

## Choose the right export

- Use `Export records` for structured data review, analysis, and exchange.
- Use `Export project` with `JSON.GZ` for a records-only upload over limited
  internet. Use ZIP or TAR.GZ when media or a complete project copy is needed.
- Use `Bundle records` for publishing, standards-based exchange, or a
  reproducible data package.
- Use `Backup database` when you need a restorable copy of the NAHPU database.

See the [Data Curation guide](../../curatorial-guideline/data-curation) and
[Backup and Restore guide](../../curatorial-guideline/backup-restore) for the
detailed workflows.
